// Package pricing turns token counts into dollars for the harnesses that do not
// report their own.
//
// Claude Code hands `tacit statusline` the session's real cost, so on that one
// client the money on the Usage page is measured. The agent routes nine
// harnesses. The other eight report tokens and no dollars, so a member on Codex
// or Cursor was reading a blank where the cost should be.
//
// A table of published rates closes it, the way ccusage does. The rates are
// generated by cmd/tacit-genprices from the providers' own published pricing,
// so the authority is the provider rather than anything written down here.
//
// The cost of that is honesty about two things, and both are enforced here
// rather than left to the caller:
//
//  1. AN ESTIMATE IS NOT A MEASUREMENT. Estimate returns a figure and the page
//     keeps it in its own field, never added to what a harness measured. A total
//     that mixed the two silently would be the most expensive kind of wrong
//     number on this page: specific, plausible and unattributable.
//
//  2. A PRICE GOES STALE. AsOf is the day this table was read off the vendors'
//     own pages, and the page prints it. A model with no row prices NOTHING
//     rather than borrowing a neighbour's rate, and a vendor whose published
//     price is a RANGE — Gemini's Pro tiers, which vary with prompt size — is
//     left out for the same reason: half a price is not a cheaper price, it is
//     a guess.
//
// Rates are USD per million tokens, keyed by modelid.Key so a dated build, a
// gateway route and a Bedrock arn all find the same row.
package pricing

import "github.com/opentacit/tacit/internal/modelid"

// AsOf is the day the prices in force were read. It is rendered beside every
// estimate, because a member comparing this month against last needs to know
// whether the prices moved under them.
//
// It reports the SNAPSHOT's day (published.go), which is the one a schedule can
// keep current. handAsOf is the day the fallback table below was last checked
// against the vendors' own pages, and it only answers where the snapshot is
// silent.
var AsOf = asOf()

const handAsOf = "2026-09-12"

func asOf() string {
	if published.AsOf != "" {
		return published.AsOf
	}
	return handAsOf
}

// Rate is one model's published price in USD per million tokens.
//
// Four numbers rather than two, because the input side is not one price. A cache
// read costs a tenth of fresh input on most models and a cache write costs a
// premium over it, and on a session that is 94% cache reads the difference
// between pricing the split and pricing the sum is an order of magnitude.
type Rate struct {
	In         float64 `json:"in"`          // fresh input, the part that missed the cache
	CacheWrite float64 `json:"cache_write"` // written into the cache
	CacheRead  float64 `json:"cache_read"`  // served from it
	Out        float64 `json:"out"`
}

// rates is the FALLBACK table, keyed by modelid.Key: the models the generated
// snapshot does not carry, and the answer of last resort if that file ever
// fails to parse. The snapshot is checked first (published.go).
//
// Anthropic: platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing. Cache writes are
// the FIVE-MINUTE rate (1.25x input). The transcript records one cache-creation
// count and does not say which TTL it was written at, so a session using the
// one-hour cache (2x) is estimated low. Named here because an unstated
// assumption in a cost figure is the kind of thing that gets found out.
//
// OpenAI: developers.openai.com/api/docs/pricing, which now prints a cache
// writes column of its own at 1.25x input. It did not always: these rows read
// "a write is billed as fresh input" long after that stopped being true, which
// is the whole argument for generating the snapshot rather than typing it.
//
// Google: ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/pricing, context-cache rate as the read.
// Only the models with ONE published rate are here; the Pro tiers and the newer
// Flash builds are priced in ranges that depend on prompt size, and a range
// cannot be resolved from a token count.
